CE\\\" . --include=\\\"*.py\\\" 2>/dev/null | head\",\n  \"description\": \"Recheck seed and findings evidence\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"seed_hex e028494f61d2d8438d8672b82d41405aa43197ab1ae8616ea3bf8c274292a560 SEED8 e028494f\\nranked ['muse-wonder-full-1.0', 'gemini-4-argon-high', 'claude-sonnet-5-5-high', 'gpt-6.1-sol-high', 'grok-4.7-high']\\nclaude-sonnet-5-5-high 5907739949cf8f9d\\ngemini-4-argon-high 2f5f860418923d51\\ngpt-6.1-sol-high b5c5f48af9dc8180\\ngrok-4.7-high bd56cc585e98dc0b\\nmuse-wonder-full-1.0 0a4a5d348d6345fc\\n===\\n      --range <RANGE>\\n          When specified, Ruff will try to only format the code in the given range.\\n          It might be necessary to extend the start backwards or the end forwards, to fully enclose\\n          a logical line.\\n          The `<RANGE>` uses the format `<start_line>:<start_column>-<end_line>:<end_column>`.\\n          \\n          - The line and column numbers are 1 based.\\n--\\n          - The column numbers are optional. You can write `--range=1-2` instead of\\n          `--range=1:1-2:1`.\\n          - The end position is optional. You can write `--range=2` to format the entire document\\n          starting from the second line.\\n          - The start position is optional. You can write `--range=-3` to format the first three\\n          lines of the document.\\n          \\n          The option can only be used when formatting a single file. Range formatting of notebooks\\n          is unsupported.\\n\\nLog levels:\\n===\\n../../../../../../bridges/opencode-zen-bridge/tests/test_catalog_mode.py\\n.\\n./probes\\n./probes/claude-sonnet-5-5-high.d\\n./seats\\n./seats/1\\n./seats/3\\n./seats/2\\n/Users/aazhiry1/tmp/cli-agent-bridge/zen-realtime-reasoning/.ai-team/runs/analyst-teamlead-zen-realtime-reasoning-20261025/panel/arch/r7\\n64:        self.assertEqual(server.CATALOG_SOURCE, \\\"static\\\")\\n93:        \\\"import json, server; print(json.dumps(